from pathlib import Path
import json,csv,html
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent;P=R/'practical-packs/production-workflows'
meta=json.loads((P/'verification.json').read_text())
def page(name,title,body):
 (P/name).write_text(f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en-AU"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{html.escape(title)} · AIT workflows</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css"><style>.table-scroll,pre{{overflow:auto}}video{{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}}audio{{max-width:100%}}.evidence{{border-left:4px solid #9d650e;padding:14px;background:#fff1c2}}@media print{{nav,video,audio,.no-print{{display:none}}}}</style></head><body><main><nav aria-label="Textbook navigation"><a href="../../index.html#home">Textbook home</a> · <a href="../index.html">Practical library</a> · <a href="index.html">Workflow pack</a></nav><h1>{html.escape(title)}</h1>{body}<p class="muted">Original teaching assets; synthetic voice and invented event details. Reference generation checked 27 October 2026. Record your application's version and actual observations.</p></main></body></html>''',encoding='utf-8')
rows=''.join(f'<tr><td><a href="exports/{name}" download>{name}</a></td><td>{m["format"]}</td><td>800 × 500</td><td>{m["bytes"]:,}</td></tr>' for name,m in meta['image_exports'].items())
page('formats.html','Export, reopen and compare media formats',f'''<p>Units 1–2 applications/data · A smaller file is useful only if it preserves the information needed by the client.</p><p><a href="source/repair-cafe-master.png" download>1600 × 1000 raster source</a> · <a href="source/reuse-mark.svg" download>Editable vector mark</a> · <a href="operation-record.csv" download>Blank operation record</a> · <a href="verification.json">Measured reference metadata</a></p><section><h2>Worked image operation</h2><ol><li>Keep the master unchanged. Open a copy in your approved image editor; record its 1600 × 1000 pixel dimensions.</li><li>Resize with resampling to 800 × 500. Preserve the whole content and aspect ratio. This operation changes sampling; cropping would remove part of the content.</li><li>Export PNG and JPEG delivery copies. For the JPEG reference, the command-line encoder used quality 75; application quality scales are not interchangeable.</li><li>Reopen each exported file. Inspect dimensions, actual bytes, event text at 100% and visible edges. Record observed differences and whether the intended brief is met.</li><li>Preserve the editable source. Renaming .jpg to .png does not perform conversion.</li></ol><p>The reference exports below were actually produced and reopened with Pillow. These are measured results for these settings, not universal format size rankings.</p><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Actual export measurements"><table><thead><tr><th>Download</th><th>Format</th><th>Pixels</th><th>Bytes</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table></div><img src="exports/campaign-800.jpg" width="800" height="500" alt="Reference repair café graphic resized to 800 by 500 pixels; compare the downloaded formats at actual size."></section><section><h2>Named formats and their limits</h2><p>PNG is lossless for its stored pixels; JPEG is commonly lossy. GIF uses a palette, so reducing full-colour source values to that palette can lose information even though GIF's compression is lossless for the palette-indexed pixels. The supplied GIF is a comparison case, not a recommended photograph master. BMP is large here; TIFF's LZW encoding is lossless for its stored pixels. Neither extension establishes print suitability.</p><p>SVG retains editable vector paths in the supplied mark; embedded raster content would still have a finite resolution. CGM is a named legacy vector format used in technical workflows. No CGM conversion or target-application support is claimed here: if the client requires CGM, use an approved supporting application and validate a supplied sample before promising delivery.</p></section><section><h2>Worked audio comparison</h2><p><a href="exports/mix-reference.wav" download>PCM WAV</a> · <a href="exports/mix-reference.mp3" download>128 kb/s MP3</a> · <a href="exports/mix-reference.wma" download>128 kb/s WMA</a></p><p>These delivery files were actually encoded from the same mix. WAV is a container; this reference uses 16-bit PCM at 44,100 samples/s. MP3 and this WMA use lossy encoding. Reopen and listen on your intended player; WMA support varies and the browser may not play it. Encoding delay and padding can make reported lossy durations differ slightly. A successful decode does not establish intelligibility.</p><audio controls preload="none" src="exports/mix-reference.mp3">Download the MP3 using the link above.</audio><p><a href="source/narration-script.txt">Transcript and source script</a>; the event information is also shown in the video workflow.</p></section><section><h2>Guided then independent work</h2><p>Predict which file is largest, then compare actual bytes. Explain why a compressed TIFF can still be larger than this PNG. For a new brief, export a 640 × 400 image below 60,000 bytes with all event text readable; choose and justify the format, record settings and revise if either check fails. There is no single required quality slider value.</p><details><summary>Check reasoning</summary><p>The reference BMP is largest at 1,200,054 bytes. Compression effectiveness depends on image content and encoding settings. The independent export must be measured; matching the size limit alone cannot compensate for unreadable information.</p></details></section>''')
page('animation.html','Build and verify a two-second animation','''<p>Unit 2 applications · Use your approved animation tool or the <a href="../animation-multimedia/animation.html">editable browser animation starter</a>.</p><p><a href="animation-plan.json" download>Timing specification</a> · <a href="exports/motion-24.gif" download>Actual 24-frame reference GIF</a> · <a href="operation-record.csv" download>Operation record</a></p><section><h2>Worked construction</h2><ol><li>Create a 640 × 180 canvas and a 24-frame sequence. Keep the horizontal guide still; move the marker from the left to the right over the sequence.</li><li>For a frame-by-frame version, explicitly set the marker positions. For a tween, define start/end positions and have the tool calculate intermediate ones. Compare the midpoint and end state.</li><li>Set nominal playback to 12 fps: 24/12 = 2 seconds. Save the editable source with its frame/timing information.</li><li>Export, reopen and verify the frame count and loop duration. Inspect start, middle and end; do not infer success from an export-complete message.</li></ol><p>The actual reference GIF has 24 frames and a 2000 ms cycle. GIF timing uses hundredths of a second: this file repeats 80, 80 and 90 ms holds eight times. Its average duration is two seconds, but each frame is not held for exactly 1/12 second. A video export at fixed 12 fps has a different timing representation.</p><p><a href="exports/motion-24.gif">Open the motion example when ready</a>. Motion is not played automatically on this guide; download or open the example by choice.</p></section><section><h2>Guided check</h2><p>What happens if a tool rounds every frame to 80 ms?</p><details><summary>Compare reasoning</summary><p>24 × 80 = 1920 ms: the loop is 0.08 seconds short. Check actual stored durations rather than assuming an fps label survives every export format.</p></details></section><section><h2>Independent transfer</h2><p>Create a three-second version with the same path, add a useful replay control in the linked browser starter or an approved interactive tool, and verify its end state and total duration. Keep your source, export, timing calculation and observed playback notes.</p></section>''')
page('multitrack.html','Edit, mix, export and inspect a publication','''<p>Unit 3 applications · Three four-second cards, separate title decisions and two audio sources.</p><p><a href="timeline-plan.json" download>Editable timeline specification</a> · <a href="source/narration-reference.wav" download>Voice track</a> · <a href="source/ambience-tone.wav" download>Supporting tone</a> · <a href="source/narration-script.txt">Voice transcript</a> · <a href="operation-record.csv" download>Blank record</a></p><p>Source cards: <a href="source/scene-01.png" download>1</a> · <a href="source/scene-02.png" download>2</a> · <a href="source/scene-03.png" download>3</a>. Synthetic voice and generated supporting tone are teaching assets.</p><section><h2>Worked sequence and decisions</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Timeline decisions"><table><thead><tr><th>Seconds</th><th>Picture and added title</th><th>Sound and effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–4</td><td>Card 1; Repair cafe</td><td>Voice gain 0.85; tone 0.12</td></tr><tr><td>4–8</td><td>Card 2; Bring a small item</td><td>Continuous separate voice and tone</td></tr><tr><td>8–12</td><td>Card 3; Hall 2 | Saturday 10 am</td><td>Tone fades during seconds 10–12</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The reference uses 0.2-second fades to/from black within each card, not overlapping dissolves. At 30 fps, 12 seconds contains 360 frames. Titles are rendered in a separate bottom panel so they do not obscure the existing card text.</p></section><section><h2>Shotcut or equivalent editor pathway</h2><ol><li>Create and save a 1280 × 720, 30 fps project. Import the three source cards onto the video timeline, each lasting four seconds.</li><li>Add the planned title text using your editor's text filter or title layer. Check all three title intervals and contrast; preserve editable text in the project.</li><li>Add separate voice and supporting-tone audio tracks starting at zero. Keep them editable. Reduce the tone and listen with the voice; the reference's linear gains are an example, not a universal loudness target.</li><li>Apply the final two-second tone fade and purposeful card fades. If using a dissolve instead, overlaps can alter duration; recheck all timing.</li><li>Save the editor-native project, then export from Timeline to a supported MP4 using H.264 video and AAC audio. Reopen the delivered file rather than playing only the editor preview.</li><li>Check 1280 × 720, intended 12-second duration, all titles, intelligible voice, no clipping and expected fade. Retain the transcript and event text alternative.</li></ol><p><a href="https://shotcut.org/stockmedia/Shotcut%20User%20Guide.pdf">Official Shotcut guide</a> · <a href="https://support.audacityteam.org/basics/saving-and-exporting-projects">Audacity project/export distinction</a>. Record the installed version; menu wording may differ.</p></section><section><h2>Inspect an actual exported reference</h2><video controls preload="metadata" width="1280" height="720" aria-label="Twelve-second repair café reference publication"><source src="exports/campaign-reference.mp4" type="video/mp4"><p><a href="exports/campaign-reference.mp4">Download the video</a></p></video><p><a href="exports/campaign-reference.mp4" download>Download MP4</a> · <a href="exports/mix-reference.wav" download>Rendered two-source mix</a> · <a href="verification.json">Measurements</a></p><p><strong>Text alternative:</strong> Repair cafe. Bring a small item. Hall 2, Saturday at 10 am. The three cards communicate those event details with synthetic narration and a supporting tone. Full narration wording is in the linked transcript.</p><p class="evidence">Actually generated with Pillow/FFmpeg: 360 decoded video frames, 1280 × 720, 30 fps, 12 seconds; a 12-second PCM mix with no samples at the clipping limit. This proves bounded export/decoding checks, not an observed Shotcut open/save session, human intelligibility judgement or accessibility approval. The JSON specification is not an editor-native project.</p></section><section><h2>Guided and independent transfer</h2><p>Mute the supporting track in your project and compare the message. Explain what remains editable in the project that is merged in the delivery copy. Then create a 16-second version with a new title, a changed middle card and separate audio. Check the longer title for clipping, recalculate frame count, and retest the actual export.</p><details><summary>Check the calculation</summary><p>16 × 30 = 480 video frames. Extending the picture timeline alone does not ensure that title duration, narration, silence and fades still serve the message.</p></details><p>Complete your publication using the <a href="../media-starter/templates/session-template.html">editable linked-page template</a> and <a href="../publication-workflow/index.html">profile/export workflow</a>. Add your delivery link, preserve keyboard navigation, make two template variants and record output conditions. Printer-specific approval remains a separate check.</p></section>''')
page('recovery.html','Perform and verify a disposable-file restore','''<p>Unit 2 managing data · The source and backup are invented records. Work only in a new practice folder containing your copies.</p><p><a href="recovery/approved-v1.csv" download>Approved version 1</a> · <a href="recovery/working-v2.csv" download>Working version 2</a> · <a href="recovery/approved-backup.zip" download>Independent ZIP backup of version 1</a> · <a href="recovery/observed-restore.json">Observed automated restore record</a> · <a href="operation-record.csv" download>Blank learner log</a></p><section><h2>Worked operation using ordinary file tools</h2><ol><li>Create a new folder for this exercise. Copy both supplied CSVs into it; retain the downloads unchanged.</li><li>Open each copy in a text editor. Version 1 says Hall 2, capacity 12. Version 2 says Hall 3, capacity 16. Record which version is approved and which change would be lost by an older restore.</li><li>Extract the supplied backup ZIP into a separate new restore folder. Inspect the file inside: it is approved-v1.csv, not the working version 2.</li><li>Compare the restored contents with the approved original. If available, calculate a SHA-256 digest of both files to compare bytes; the observed reference record supplies the expected digests.</li><li>Keep the restored copy and version 2 separate. Decide whether to retain, reconcile or seek approval for the later change; do not overwrite the only newer copy.</li></ol><p class="evidence">A real disposable-file test created a ZIP, replaced its temporary working file and restored version 1. Its restored digest matches version 1 and differs from version 2. The test establishes restoration of those bytes, not that version 1 is the latest, that sync is backup, or that a production recovery plan works.</p></section><section><h2>Maintenance: inspect before acting</h2><p>Use the <a href="../maintenance-lab/guide.html">existing supervised OS-utility pathway</a> in a teacher-approved training environment. Record OS version, drive type, utility, selected category, preview, approval, actual result and limitation. Preserve the fixture sources and backup. This pack does not instruct learners to clean the host machine or install utilities.</p><p>For Windows, <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/storage-filemanagement/manage-drive-space-with-storage-sense">Microsoft's Storage Sense guidance</a> describes settings and their scope. A drive report, cleanup, optimisation and protection scan are different operations. No actual OS cleanup or malware scan was performed for this pack.</p></section><section><h2>Guided and independent transfer</h2><p>Does a successful version-1 restore recover the capacity-16 edit?</p><details><summary>Compare reasoning</summary><p>No. The backup point predates that edit. Verify both byte integrity and whether the recovery point meets the user's need.</p></details><p>Create an approved version 3 in your practice folder, make an independent backup, edit a second working copy, then restore into a new folder. Compare contents/digests and explain the recovery-point limit. Keep the backup, restored file and observed log; label unperformed OS-utility steps unverified.</p></section>''')
with (P/'operation-record.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf-8') as f:
 w=csv.writer(f);w.writerow(['workflow','application_version','source','operation_settings','expected','observed','evidence_filename','decision_or_revision','retest','limitation']);w.writerow(['','','','','','','','','',''])
page('index.html','Practical workflows: make, inspect and revise','''<p>Use real inputs and measured reference outputs, then produce an independent result with your approved classroom tools.</p><p><a class="button" href="../production-workflows-v1.zip" download>Download all workflow inputs and references</a> <a href="operation-record.csv" download>Blank observation record</a></p><section><h2>1. Image and audio delivery · Units 1–2</h2><p><a class="button" href="formats.html">Export and compare formats</a></p><p>Measured PNG/JPEG/BMP/TIFF/GIF outputs, vector source, WAV/MP3/WMA audio, and a new delivery brief.</p></section><section><h2>2. Animation · Unit 2</h2><p><a class="button" href="animation.html">Build and check a two-second sequence</a></p><p>24-frame reference, actual stored timing and a three-second independent transfer.</p></section><section><h2>3. Multi-track publication · Unit 3</h2><p><a class="button" href="multitrack.html">Edit and inspect an exported sequence</a></p><p>Picture/title decisions, separate source audio, editable specification, a real MP4 reference and native-editor steps.</p></section><section><h2>4. Version recovery and utility evidence · Unit 2</h2><p><a class="button" href="recovery.html">Restore and verify a practice copy</a></p><p>Actual ZIP backup and observed byte comparison; separate supervised maintenance pathway.</p></section><section><h2>Use within the existing lessons</h2><p>Replace the corresponding application tasks in the existing four 55-minute lessons per week; do not add a second project or use exam-reserve weeks. Keep modelling, supported practice, independent production, feedback and the exit check inside the current session allocation. Teachers select the relevant workflow and scale the new output to the available time.</p></section><section><h2>What is verified?</h2><p>Reference files were actually generated, reopened/decoded and checked. <a href="verification.json">Read the measurements and limits</a>. Desktop-editor sessions, printer-specific proofs and real OS maintenance still require their own recorded trials. Do not copy the reference's observed results into your learner log.</p></section>''')
print('Built complete student workflow pages and blank observation record.')
